WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.240
Input handling in C is security-sensitive because several concerns arrive at the same time.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.900
The program is not just deciding whether a value looks acceptable.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:21.120
It is also deciding how many bytes to read, where those bytes will live, how they become strings or numbers, what happens if parsing fails, and whether the next operation has enough memory and enough context to be safe.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:25.420
In many languages, some of those decisions are guarded by runtime checks.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:28.520
In C, the program owns those checks directly.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.180
External input should be treated as untrusted no matter where it comes from.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:47.520
Keyboard input, command-line arguments, files, network packets, device data, environment variables, configuration files, and library callbacks can all contain values that are malformed, oversized, incomplete, surprising, or intentionally hostile.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.540
A local file is not automatically trustworthy.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.000
A value supplied by another internal process is not automatically trustworthy.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:01:00.740
Every boundary deserves the same question: what exactly are we willing to accept before we use this data?

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:11.680
C does not automatically protect buffer capacity, pointer validity, integer ranges, string termination, or the relationship between a length field and the actual memory available.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:23.900
If code copies before validating, formats text into a too-small destination, trusts a count from a file, or assumes a string is null terminated, the bug may become memory corruption or unpredictable control flow.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.800
Secure input handling is therefore a defensive engineering discipline.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:36.340
Define acceptable input, validate before use, preserve lengths alongside buffers, check every return value, and make rejection paths predictable.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.800
The goal is not to make parsing complicated.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:43.820
The goal is to make every later operation depend on facts the program has actually verified.
